summary:
- topic: FY25 Budget Presentation
  text: Superintendent Angela Atkinson Duina presented the FY25 budget, emphasizing
    its focus on 'Learning for All with Excellence and Equity.' She detailed the challenging
    development process, influenced by the loss of COVID-era funds and enrollment
    variability, which necessitated difficult prioritization decisions.
- topic: Budget Financials and Tax Impact
  text: The proposed budget includes a 5% operating budget increase and a 3.82% mill
    rate increase. This translates to an estimated $123 annual increase for a median
    homeowner, which the Superintendent deemed fair to taxpayers while meeting district
    needs.
- topic: Comparative Budget Analysis
  text: South Portland's proposed operating budget increase of 5% was shown to be
    lower than those of neighboring districts like Falmouth, Gorham, Scarborough,
    and Westbrook. Portland's mill rate increase was also noted to be higher, impacting
    their median homeowner more significantly.
- topic: Health Insurance and Unfunded Positions
  text: An update on health insurance indicated a statewide increase range of 1.5%
    to 11.5%, with South Portland budgeting for 7% and awaiting specific figures.
    The board also inquired about the costs of previously unfunded positions, such
    as a mental health coordinator and an additional pre-K set, for potential future
    consideration.
- topic: Extended Learning Opportunities (ELOs) Partnership
  text: Extended Learning Opportunities at SOUTHPORTLANDHIGHSCHOOL will continue through
    a new partnership with JMG, with a JMG staff member serving as an ELO Navigator.
    This collaboration aims to optimize JMG's expertise in career-connected education
    and ensure the program's robust continuation after a grant concluded.
- topic: Equity in School Events and Sports
  text: Board members raised concerns about financial barriers to student participation
    in school events and sports. The administration committed to investigating policies
    to ensure accessibility, particularly for essential uniform items like swimsuits,
    to prevent any student from being excluded due to cost.
- topic: Budget and Calendar Approval
  text: "The board unanimously approved the FY25 Superintendent\u2019s Budget, acknowledging\
    \ the thorough process and difficult decisions involved. They also unanimously\
    \ approved a revised 2024-2025 school year calendar, which adds one day, moving\
    \ the last day of school to June 13, 2025."
